    "abstract": null,
    "field": "Global land-atmosphere energy fluxes",
    "problem": "Global land-atmosphere energy fluxes are poorly constrained due to large uncertainties in models, sparse and systematically biased in-situ measurements, and uncharacterized errors in previous data-driven upscaling efforts, which hinders the assessment of global energy and water budgets.",
    "method": "The proposed method uses machine learning to merge eddy covariance flux measurements with remote sensing and meteorological data to produce an ensemble of global gridded estimates for net radiation, latent heat, and sensible heat, along with their uncertainties from various sources like models and energy balance corrections.",
    "task": "The task is to estimate global gridded land-atmosphere energy fluxes by using machine learning to merge in-situ flux measurements with remote sensing and meteorological data, creating a comprehensive ensemble of data products called FLUXCOM.",
