    "innovation": "This paper presents the FLUXCOM database, a comprehensive ensemble of global land-atmosphere energy flux products derived by merging FLUXNET measurements with satellite and meteorological data using multiple machine learning algorithms to systematically quantify uncertainties.",
    "kg_extraction_triplets": [
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "FLUXCOM",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "machine learning",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "Although a key driver of Earth's climate system, global land-atmosphere energy fluxes are poorly constrained. Here we use machine learning to merge energy flux measurements from FLUXNET eddy covariance towers with remote sensing and meteorological data to estimate global gridded net radiation, latent and sensible heat and their uncertainties. The resulting FLUXCOM database comprises 147 products in two setups: (1) 0.0833\u00b0 resolution using MODIS remote sensing data (RS) and (2) 0.5\u00b0 resolution using remote sensing and meteorological data (RS + METEO). Within each setup we use a full factorial design across machine learning methods, forcing datasets and energy balance closure corrections.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "GLEAM",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "Priestley-Taylor formulation",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "For LE, we compare FLUXCOM RS and RS + METEO ensembles against those from Model Tree Ensemble (MTE10), the Global Land Evaporation Amsterdam Model (GLEAM v3.1a40), and LandFlux-EVAL41. The MTE is based on only one machine learning methods trained on monthly flux data 9,10 and may be regarded as a precursor to FLUXCOM. GLEAM (https://www.gleam.eu/) models evapotranspiration based on a Priestley-Taylor formulation42 with explicit soil moisture stress, and the interception by the Gash model43, and was informed by various satellite forcing data.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "GLEAM",
